{"author":{"id":"1949ffc23f11a8cb4f8c42a45c9ad241b49570df9a18687489e9ea78d87c54a3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774344305,"nonce":"ppky1GxszVFIcNanENWZu+mLPhU=","title":"model.net and model.nodes['net'] are different PyTree objects — training updates wrong copy","message":"## Summary\n\nIn the Graph architecture, model.net (a dataclass field) and model.nodes['net'] (the entry in the nodes dict) are DIFFERENT Python objects. They have the same values but different identities. This means:\n\n1. Graph._execute_step iterates over self.nodes — uses nodes['net'] for the forward pass\n2. where_train resolves \"net.hidden\" via attrgetter → model.net.hidden — selects the FIELD copy\n3. eqx.partition marks the FIELD copy's leaves as trainable\n4. Gradients flow through the FIELD copy, not the NODES copy\n5. Parameter updates modify the FIELD copy, which is never used in the forward pass\n6. The model never learns\n\n## Evidence\n\nmodel.net is model.nodes['net'] → False\nmodel.net id: 4994761984, model.nodes['net'] id: 4971475696\nLeaves: same count (27), values equal, identities differ\n\nGradient norm via model.net path: 0.0 (zero — wrong leaves)\nGradient norm via model.nodes['net'] path: 0.43 (correct)\n\n## Workaround\n\nUse a custom where function that accesses model.nodes['net'] directly instead of model.net.\n\n## Proper Fix\n\nEither:\n1. model.net should be a @property that returns model.nodes['net'] (not a separate field)\n2. Or where_strs_to_fns should resolve through nodes dict, not attrgetter\n3. Or the Graph class should ensure that the field and nodes entry are the same object\n\nThis is a critical training correctness bug.\n\nCo-authored-by: Claude Opus 4.6 \u003cnoreply@anthropic.com\u003e","files":null}]}